import CombinatorialGroupTheory_Magnus_2004.Basic
import CombinatorialGroupTheory_Magnus_2004.Chap01.Proposition_1_6_13

-- Declarations for this item will be appended below by the statement pipeline.

open FreeGroup
open scoped RelatorSetIr

-- Layer triage:
-- `source-facing`: a singleton relator set `W = {w}` in the free group on `n + 1` generators,
-- where `w` is obtained by conjugating a word in the first `n` generators by a positive power of
-- the last generator, together with the textbook hypotheses that the inner word lies in no basis
-- and is not a proper power in that smaller free group.
-- `core/canonical`: the relator-set owner notation `Ir(W)`, the presented-group owner
-- `PresentedGroup W` behind that definition, the primitive-element owner `IsPrimitiveElement`, the
-- automorphism owner `MulAut.conj`, and the canonical inclusion
-- `map Fin.castSucc : FreeGroup (Fin n) →* FreeGroup (Fin n.succ)`.
-- `bridge/view`: the textbook generators `ξ₁, …, ξₙ` are represented by `Fin n.succ`; the inner
-- word `w₁` lives in `FreeGroup (Fin n)` and is viewed in the ambient rank-`n + 1` free group via
-- `map Fin.castSucc`, while the source phrase “lies in no basis” is expressed canonically by
-- `¬ IsPrimitiveElement w₁`; the outer conjugation is then expressed by `MulAut.conj`, and the
-- source phrase “`w₁` is not a proper power” is expressed by
-- `¬ IsProperPower w₁`.
-- Domain sampling:
-- 1. `Ir(W)` from Proposition `1-6-13` is the chapter owner abstraction for irreducibility rank.
-- 2. `PresentedGroup` in mathlib is the canonical owner object on which `Ir(W)` is built.
-- 3. `IsPrimitiveElement` in `CombinatorialGroupTheory.Basic` is the project owner abstraction
--    for “belongs to some basis”, so its negation is the canonical owner-level form of “lies in
--    no basis”.
-- 4. `map Fin.castSucc` in mathlib is the canonical way to view a word in the first `n`
--    generators inside the free group on `n + 1` generators.
-- Primitive vs. derived:
-- the primitive source data are the inner word `w₁`, its non-primitive and non-proper-power
-- hypotheses in the smaller free group, and the exponent `k`; the singleton relator set in the
-- ambient free group is derived canonically from those data via `map Fin.castSucc` and
-- `MulAut.conj`.

/-- Proposition 1-6-12: let `W` be the singleton relator set in the free group on `n + 1`
generators generated by
`w = ξ_(n+1)^(-k) * w₁(ξ₁, …, ξ_n) * ξ_(n+1)^k`, with `k > 1`, where `w₁` lies in no basis of the
smaller free group on `ξ₁, …, ξ_n` and is not a proper power there. Then `Ir(W) < n`. -/
-- Proof sketch: this is the chapter owner-form of the textbook irreducibility statement for a
-- one-relator presentation whose relator is a conjugate of a non-primitive, non-proper-power word
-- from the first `n` generators. The primitive hypotheses are stated on the inner word in its
-- natural smaller-rank owner, while the ambient singleton relator is derived canonically via
-- `map Fin.castSucc` and `MulAut.conj`; the conclusion is stated directly in terms of `Ir(W)`.
theorem ir_singleton_conjugate_lt_of_nonprimitive_not_properPower
    (n : ℕ)
    (w₁ : FreeGroup (Fin n))
    (hw₁_nonprimitive : ¬ IsPrimitiveElement w₁)
    (hw₁_not_properPower : ¬ IsProperPower w₁)
    (k : ℕ)
    (hk : 1 < k) :
    Ir(({MulAut.conj (((of (Fin.last n)) ^ k)⁻¹) (map Fin.castSucc w₁)} :
      Set (FreeGroup (Fin n.succ)))) < n := sorry
